{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "After the button had been pressed and the hand reopened at its hover, the next action was an open-hand hover over the container concealing the requested cube. The retained uncovered_scene identified that container, button_pressed was true, and completed_pickups was 0. Nevertheless, the policy misplaced the foreground green-target hover substantially along world x; another red-target hover had a smaller combined horizontal error exceeding 1 cm. These are waypoint SUFFICIENCY failures in geometric reconstruction, not missing historical information or memory-transition failures. Strengthen the existing calibrated top-face reconstruction rule with the explicit calculation: for each pixel corner p=[u,v,1] of the same horizontal target top face, use the relevant camera's world-to-camera extrinsics [R|t] to compute camera center C=-R^T t and world ray direction d=R^T K^-1 p. Intersect with the top/grasp plane h approximately 0.0634 m using X=C+((h-C_z)/d_z)d. Average the reconstructed coplanar corners to obtain target_x and target_y, and command the open-hand hover at [target_x,target_y,0.20]. Use the current wrist extrinsics for wrist pixels and the fixed front extrinsics for front pixels. Reproject the reconstructed square into the available current views to check that its corners and edges match the selected top face; resolve inconsistent corner selections or arithmetic before issuing the waypoint. Do not approximate world x from image row, use a table-plane intersection for an elevated face, or combine side-wall corners with top-face corners. Preserve the existing target-identity, square-aligned yaw, descent, and stage-selection rules. No additional memory field or episode-specific coordinate rule is needed."
}

